# Test automation contract

1. **Independent state and oracle.** Each test declares setup and cleanup through
   the runner's fixture conventions. No ordering dependence or shared mutable
   account. Test login through UI when login is the subject; other tests may use
   safe API/session setup. Signup tests may create disposable accounts.
2. **Observed locators.** Prefer semantic role/name or accessible identifiers and
   explicit test IDs supported by the product. Prove uniqueness and behavior in
   the tested DOM/accessibility tree/build; record source provenance when source
   exists. Text is valid when it is the intended observable contract, with locale
   controlled. Avoid brittle positional selectors. Never invent identifiers or
   pretend source inspection proves the installed build. A locator registry is
   optional; missing IDs alone do not block usable semantic UI automation.
3. **Risk before volume.** Link each test to a criterion or failure risk; assert
   observable outcomes, not the implementation's own calculation repeated back.
   Prefer the lowest effective layer, retain real integration/end-to-end paths,
   and state what stubs/mocks cannot establish.
4. **Condition-based synchronization.** Wait on observable state with a timeout
   and actionable diagnostic. Do not add arbitrary sleeps or increase timeouts to
   hide failures. Timing may be an input when testing timing behavior itself.
5. **Isolated non-production targets.** Verify target identity before mutation.
   Use least-privilege test identities, run-specific data and task-owned cleanup.
   Never derive a safety claim from an environment's label alone.
6. **Preserve failures.** No rerun-until-green. One diagnostic rerun at most for
   an explicit hypothesis; retain both outputs. Assertion, product, fixture,
   locator and infrastructure classifications require evidence.
7. **Expected failures stay visible.** A quarantine/xfail needs a linked defect,
   owner, reason and review date. Use strict unexpected-pass behavior if supported;
   otherwise inspect/report it separately. Do not silently enable runner retries
   that mask first failures or claim known-broken criteria passed.
8. **Respect the source of truth.** Git-managed tests/collections are edited in
   Git. Cloud-managed collections are edited through the authorized service and
   synced. Determine ownership first; neither model is universal.
9. **Verify what exists.** Run the repository's actual documented checks. Record
   command, cwd, target/build and exit status with redacted artifacts. Zero tests,
   skipped-only runs and collection parsing are not a testing Pass.
10. **Keep evidence safe and useful.** Do not hide failed checks, weaken assertions,
    copy credentials/customer data into logs, or upload artifacts outside approved
    storage. Retain full redacted output outside the prompt; quote decisive lines.
